GROWTH GAPS

1. High-Income & Micro-Agency Frameworks
While you focus on providing free resources (e.g., free Claude tokens, free certificates), top-performing competitors like @dhavalkataria_ (19.21% engagement) and @aryamanupmanyu (2.56% engagement) focus on monetization and business building. Dhaval hooks viewers with raw financial figures ("This one video made me 16 lakh rupees", "If you are not making 1 lakh a month..."), while Aryaman breaks down specific, actionable AI automation businesses (e.g., AI Legal Agency, Real Estate Property Intelligence). You are missing out on the high-intent, aspirational audience that is obsessed with making money using AI.

2. Rapid-Fire Tool Comparison Matrices
Competitors like @nivedan.ai leverage a highly structured, visual comparison format ("For writing, this is bad, this is okay, this is great... For building websites, this is terrible, this is fine..."). This matrix style of content has high visual retention because it simplifies complex AI landscapes into immediate "winners" and "losers." Currently, your tool reviews are highly informative but lack this rapid-fire, visually structured comparison hook.

3. Capitalizing on the "Vibe Coding" Trend
Both @arshman and @nick_saraev are driving huge traction by targeting "vibe coding" (using AI to build applications without deep coding knowledge). @arshman uses relatable hooks like "I can spot a vibe-coded website in 3 seconds" to establish authority, while @nick_saraev breaks down open-source security tools for vibe-coded apps. Although you cover Claude Code, you are not directly positioning your content around the culturally relevant "Vibe Coding" trend, which is highly searchable and viral right now.


